    {
      "argv": [
        "node",
        "/Users/brad/.cache/swarm-comparison/tools/swarm-6f4984002/dist/cli.js",
        "chai's `.oneOf(list)` assertion (function oneOf in lib/chai/core/assertions.js) has a `contain`/`include` form that asserts the subject array or string contains at least one member of the list. That form only uses strict identity via indexOf, so the `deep` flag has no effect: expect([[1], [2]]).to.deep.contain.oneOf([[1], [2]]) currently fails with \"expected [ [ 1 ], [ 2 ] ] to contain one of [ [ 1 ], [ 2 ] ]\". Add deep support to the contain form. When the `deep` flag is set and the subject is an array, a list member counts as contained if any subject element is deep-equal to it (use the same deep-equality utility the file already uses for other deep assertions); without the deep flag the current strict behavior must be unchanged, so expect([[1]]).to.not.contain.oneOf([[1]]) still passes. The failure messages for the contain form must say \"deeply\" when the deep flag is set: expect([[1]]).to.deep.contain.oneOf([[2]]) must throw \"expected [ [ 1 ] ] to deeply contain one of [ [ 2 ] ]\" and expect([[1]]).to.not.deep.contain.oneOf([[1]]) must throw \"expected [ [ 1 ] ] to not deeply contain one of [ [ 1 ] ]\". Nested cases such as expect([[[1]]]).to.deep.contain.oneOf([[[1]]]) must pass. String subjects, the non-contain form of oneOf, the list-must-be-an-array check and all existing messages stay as they are. Do not edit the test directory.\n\nVisible acceptance check (it must pass; it is not the only criterion): npm run build && node acceptance/visible/check.mjs\nThe project's own tests run with: npm run test-node\nDo not modify anything under acceptance/visible/.",
